    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## `n_jobs`\n",
        "You may assign `n_jobs` for parallel computing. You may leave it `None` right now. We will come back to it later when we really need it.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Modern Hyperparameter Tuning\n",
        "\n",
        "Historically, one of the most common approaches to hyperparameter tuning was exhaustive grid search, which is introduced above. This approach is conceptually simple and works well when the number of hyperparameters is small. As a result, `GridSearchCV` became one of the standard tools in early machine learning workflows.\n",
        "\n",
        "However, as models became more complicated, researchers observed that exhaustive grid search often wastes substantial computation. In many problems, only a few hyperparameters strongly affect performance, while others have relatively small influence.\n",
        "\n",
        "A particularly influential paper @Bergstra2012 by Bergstra and Bengio showed that random search can often outperform grid search in high-dimensional hyperparameter spaces. The main idea is that random search explores more distinct values along important hyperparameter directions instead of spending excessive computation on unimportant dimensions.\n",
        "\n",
        "This led to the widespread use of methods such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- `RandomizedSearchCV`\n",
        "- Bayesian optimization\n",
        "- TPE (Tree-structured Parzen Estimator)\n",
        "- Optuna\n",
        "- AutoML frameworks\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern frameworks such as `Optuna` use adaptive search strategies. Instead of testing hyperparameters independently, they use information from previous trials to guide future searches toward more promising regions of the hyperparameter space. These methods have become increasingly important for large and computationally expensive models.\n",
        "\n",
        "Nevertheless, in this course we will mainly use ordinary grid search because it is simple, transparent, and easy to understand. For most models in this course, naive grid search is already sufficient.\n",
        "\n",
        "Only for more computationally expensive models such as XGBoost will we occasionally use more practical workflows such as:\n",
        "\n",
        "- random search followed by refined grid search\n",
        "- stagewise tuning\n",
        "- early stopping\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Feature Engineering and Preprocessing\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Feature engineering refers to modifying or creating predictors in order to better represent the relationship between the predictors and the response. Here we mainly cover the `sklearn` way to perform feature engineering.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Modeling Perspective\n",
        "\n",
        "In statistical learning (commonly implemented using `sklearn`), the primary goal is predictive performance. Models are therefore typically constructed by building a feature matrix, and modifying a model usually means modifying the feature matrix rather than changing a model formula.\n",
        "\n",
        "In contrast, traditional regression analysis (commonly implemented using `statsmodels`) allows explicit specification of statistical models through a formula language. This approach is convenient when precise control of model terms and statistical inference are required.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Creating Features Manually\n",
        "\n",
        "Before introducing automated tools, we can always create new predictors directly by modifying the original dataset."
      ],
      "id": "8b2bd7be"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "X = pd.DataFrame({'x1': [0, 2, 4], 'x2': [1, 3, 5]})\n",
        "X"
      ],
      "id": "aae25d9a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "For example, we can add the squared term of $x_2$ and the interaction term $x_1x_2$ to form a new feature matrix `X2`.\n"
      ],
      "id": "32e4bb5d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X2 = X.copy()\n",
        "X2[\"x2_sq\"] = X[\"x2\"] ** 2\n",
        "X2[\"x1_x2\"] = X[\"x1\"] * X[\"x2\"]\n",
        "X2"
      ],
      "id": "6a1697e8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This corresponds to fitting a model of the form\n",
        "$$\n",
        "y=\\beta_0+\\beta_1x_1+\\beta_2x_2+\\beta_3x_2^2+\\beta_4x_1x_2+\\varepsilon.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "Although the model is called linear regression, it can represent nonlinear relationships by including transformed predictors such as $x^2$, $\\log x$, or $\\sqrt{x}$. The model remains linear because it is linear in the coefficients $\\beta$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Polynomial Features\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<details>\n",
        "<summary><strong>Click to expand.</strong></summary>\n",
        "\n",
        "When many predictors are present, manually creating polynomial and interaction terms can become tedious. In such cases we can use `PolynomialFeatures` from `sklearn.preprocessing`, which automatically generates polynomial and interaction terms up to the specified degree.\n"
      ],
      "id": "a621e1c6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\n",
        "\n",
        "poly = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, interaction_only=False, include_bias=True)\n",
        "poly.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "15f5a0ab",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The argument `interaction_only=False` means that both squared terms and interaction terms are included. For example, if the original variables are $x_1$ and $x_2$, then the transformed matrix contains terms such as\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1,x_1,x_2,x_1^2,x_1x_2,x_2^2.\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "6cdacfb9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "poly2 = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, interaction_only=True, include_bias=False)\n",
        "poly2.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "f51cd2fe",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here `interaction_only=True` means that only interaction terms are added, while powers such as $x_1^2$ and $x_2^2$ are excluded. In addition, the argument `include_bias=False` removes the constant column of ones.\n",
        "</details>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Categorical Variables\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "## Dummy Variable Trap\n",
        "Including all dummy variables together with an intercept creates perfect multicollinearity because the dummy variables sum to one. Therefore, when creating dummy variables with $k$ categories, we typically include only $k-1$ variables.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<details>\n",
        "<summary><strong>`pandas` version</strong></summary>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "We could use `pd.get_dummies()` to directly modify the dataset. "
      ],
      "id": "4542524e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df = pd.DataFrame({\"color\": [\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\", \"red\"]})\n",
        "\n",
        "pd.get_dummies(df, drop_first=True)"
      ],
      "id": "9a6cc6de",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The argument `drop_first=True` removes the base category column to avoid the dummy variable trap described above. If you want to keep the base column, you may set it to `False` (which is the default).\n",
        "\n",
        "`pd.get_dummies()` automatically detects categorical variables and leaves numerical variables unchanged."
      ],
      "id": "3766602e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X = pd.DataFrame({\"x\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], \"group\": [\"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\", \"C\", \"C\"]})\n",
        "\n",
        "pd.get_dummies(X, drop_first=True)"
      ],
      "id": "45ce0fb2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "If you want to convert numeric variables into dummy variables, you may manually cast them to categorical variables."
      ],
      "id": "24490245"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X[\"x\"] = X[\"x\"].astype(\"category\")\n",
        "pd.get_dummies(X, drop_first=True)"
      ],
      "id": "4366f3a5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</details>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<details>\n",
        "<summary><strong>`sklearn` version</strong></summary>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "`OneHotEncoder` from `sklearn` is another tool for converting categorical variables into dummy variables. It follows the standard `sklearn` API. Compared with `pd.get_dummies()`, which is convenient for quick data analysis, `OneHotEncoder` is better suited for statistical learning pipelines because it can be fitted on training data and then applied consistently to new data."
      ],
      "id": "557a9b8f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\n",
        "\n",
        "df = pd.DataFrame({\"color\": [\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\", \"red\"]})\n",
        "encoder = OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\", sparse_output=False) \n",
        "\n",
        "encoder.fit_transform(df)"
      ],
      "id": "7671245d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The argument `sparse_output=False` controls the output format. If `False`, the output is a regular matrix. If `True`, the output is a sparse matrix.\n"
      ],
      "id": "ade0ff45"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder\n",
        "\n",
        "X = pd.DataFrame({\"x\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], \"group\": [\"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\", \"C\", \"C\"]})\n",
        "encoder = OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\", sparse_output=False)\n",
        "\n",
        "encoder.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "0bb7f173",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "When applied to a DataFrame with multiple columns, unlike the `pandas` version, `OneHotEncoder` converts each column into dummy variables. In the example above, the first five columns correspond to `x` and the last two correspond to `group`.\n",
        "\n",
        "If you want to apply `OneHotEncoder` only to certain columns, you need to use a `ColumnTransformer`, which will be introduced later.\n",
        "\n",
        "</details>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### Scaling Variables {#sec-scaling}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<details>\n",
        "<summary><strong>Centralization, standardization and normalization</strong></summary>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "In regression and statistical learning, these transformations are typically applied to the predictors $X$ to control their location and scale. They are rarely applied to the response $y$, unless there is a specific modeling reason.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Centralization: Subtract the sample mean so that the variable has mean $0$.\n",
        "- Standardization: Subtract the sample mean and divide by the sample standard deviation so that the variable has mean $0$ and standard deviation $1$.\n",
        "- Normalization: Rescale the variable so that its values lie between $0$ and $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Standardization is especially useful when predictors are measured on very different scales. It is commonly used in statistical learning, particularly for methods such as ridge regression and lasso.\n"
      ],
      "id": "310d9900"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "np.random.seed(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "X = pd.DataFrame({\"x1\": np.random.normal(10, 2, size=8), \"x2\": np.random.normal(100, 20, size=8)})\n",
        "X"
      ],
      "id": "4f6155a0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Standardization:"
      ],
      "id": "e3705bb6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
        "\n",
        "scaler = StandardScaler()\n",
        "scaler.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "3dd8f891",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Centralization (standardization without scaling):"
      ],
      "id": "b749cb63"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "scaler = StandardScaler(with_std=False)\n",
        "scaler.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "1457f0bb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Normalization:\n"
      ],
      "id": "b4e209e9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import MinMaxScaler\n",
        "\n",
        "minmax = MinMaxScaler()\n",
        "minmax.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "aa4f62ca",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Note that scaling is not required for ordinary least squares (OLS). However, it is essential for regularization methods such as ridge and lasso because the penalty depends on the scale of the coefficients.\n",
        "</details>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### `ColumnTransformer`\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "<details>\n",
        "<summary><strong>Click to expand.</strong></summary>\n",
        "\n",
        "To apply different transformations to different columns, we can use `ColumnTransformer`. `ColumnTransformer` consists of multiple transformations. Each transformation is specified by a triple containing:\n",
        "\n",
        "- a name (identifier),\n",
        "- the transformer,\n",
        "- the columns to which it applies.\n"
      ],
      "id": "3c749a78"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\n",
        "\n",
        "prep = ColumnTransformer(\n",
        "    transformers=[\n",
        "        (\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\", sparse_output=False), [\"group\"]),\n",
        "        (\"x\", \"passthrough\", [\"x\"]),\n",
        "        (\"x_std\", StandardScaler(), [\"x\"]),\n",
        "    ]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "X = pd.DataFrame({\"x\": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], \"group\": [\"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\", \"C\", \"C\"]})\n",
        "prep.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "330cb810",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "You may use the id to get into each transformer.\n"
      ],
      "id": "d1c7ab2e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "prep.transformers_"
      ],
      "id": "c082e8de",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "prep.named_transformers_['x_std']"
      ],
      "id": "2ca6d157",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</details>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip}\n",
        "## Estimator, Predictor and Transformer\n",
        "\n",
        "In `sklearn`, all models are called estimators. An estimator is any object that can be trained using the `.fit()` method.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Once an estimator is fitted,\n",
        "\n",
        "- if it can transform input data using `.transform()`, it is called a transformer;\n",
        "- if it can generate predictions using `.predict()`, it is called a predictor.\n",
        "\n",
        "Typical examples include\n",
        "\n",
        "- Transformers: `StandardScaler`, `MinMaxScaler`, `PolynomialFeatures`, `OneHotEncoder`, etc.\n",
        "- Predictors: `LinearRegression` and most other machine learning models.\n",
        "\n",
        "Usually a model is applied in two steps: \n",
        "\n",
        "1. `.fit()` learns the parameters from the training data.\n",
        "2. `.transform()` or `.predict()` applies the model to data.\n",
        "\n",
        "For transformers, the combined method `.fit_transform()` performs both steps in one call.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Pipelines and transformations of $y$\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "In many modeling workflows, several steps must be performed before fitting the model. These steps may include\n",
        "\n",
        "- feature transformations,\n",
        "- scaling variables,\n",
        "- generating polynomial features.\n",
        "\n",
        "A `pipeline` combines these steps into a single object. Using pipelines ensures that the same transformations are applied consistently during training, cross-validation, and prediction, and helps prevent data leakage.\n",
        "\n",
        "Here is a typical `pipeline`. Each step is indicated by a pair consisting of\n",
        "\n",
        "- a name (identifier) of the step\n",
        "- the estimator\n"
      ],
      "id": "84621d60"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
        "\n",
        "steps = [\n",
        "    (\"scaler\", StandardScaler()),\n",
        "    (\"linear_model\", LinearRegression()),\n",
        "]\n",
        "pipe = Pipeline(steps=steps)"
      ],
      "id": "d8386d00",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This `pipe` object behaves like a `model` object. You may use the step names to access the internal components.\n"
      ],
      "id": "9141b4f4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pipe.steps"
      ],
      "id": "9ffb72f6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pipe.named_steps['scaler']"
      ],
      "id": "9b3cb426",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "In a pipeline, you normally need to manually name all components. To make this easier, you may use `make_pipeline()`, which automatically generates default names for each step.\n",
        "\n",
        "In addition, `make_pipeline()` does not require a `list` of `(name, step)` pairs. Instead, you simply provide the steps one by one."
      ],
      "id": "f09cca12"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n",
        "\n",
        "pipe_easy = make_pipeline(\n",
        "    StandardScaler(),\n",
        "    LinearRegression()\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "6938aeaa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "It will automatically name each step by its class name."
      ],
      "id": "c1ffe786"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pipe_easy.named_steps"
      ],
      "id": "e7efde92",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "Pipelines are particularly useful when combined with cross-validation and hyperparameter tuning, since they ensure that preprocessing steps are applied within each training fold.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "### `TransformedTargetRegressor`\n",
        "\n",
        "`TransformedTargetRegressor` from `sklearn.compose` is a wrapper that applies a transformation to the response variable $y$ during model fitting and automatically applies the inverse transformation when making predictions. In other words, the regressor is trained on the transformed response, while predictions are converted back to the original scale.\n",
        "\n",
        "Here is a simple example of a `TransformedTargetRegressor`. The transformation of $y$ is centralizer, which is essentially standardizer without rescaling."
      ],
      "id": "e7de4504"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| eval: false\n",
        "from sklearn.compose import TransformedTargetRegressor\n",
        "\n",
        "model = TransformedTargetRegressor(\n",
        "    regressor=LinearRegression(),\n",
        "    transformer=StandardScaler(with_std=False)\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "23196b4e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Full Example\n",
        "\n",
        "<details>\n",
        "<summary><strong>Click to expand.</strong></summary>\n",
        "\n",
        "In this example we build a more complicated pipeline."
      ],
      "id": "4fd8add9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "\n",
        "np.random.seed(1)\n",
        "\n",
        "n = 100\n",
        "\n",
        "X = pd.DataFrame(\n",
        "    {\n",
        "        \"x1\": np.random.normal(0, 1, n),\n",
        "        \"x2\": np.random.normal(5, 2, n),\n",
        "        \"group\": np.random.choice([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"], size=n),\n",
        "    }\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "y = 1 + 2 * X[\"x1\"] - 1.5 * X[\"x2\"] + 3 * (X[\"group\"] == \"B\") - 2 * (X[\"group\"] == \"C\") + np.random.normal(0, 1, n)"
      ],
      "id": "eba57f5b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The pipeline is illustrated as follows.\n",
        "\n",
        "1. `ColumnTransformer` separates numeric and categorical features.\n",
        "2. Numeric features go through: `StandardScaler` → `PolynomialFeatures`.\n",
        "3. Categorical features go through `OneHotEncoder`.\n",
        "4. The transformed features are combined into a single feature matrix.\n",
        "5. The model `LinearRegression` is fitted to the transformed data.\n",
        "6. The response variable $y$ is centralized.\n",
        "7. The fitted model can then be used to generate predictions, while the transforamtion of $y$ is automatically reversed.\n",
        "\n",
        "```{mermaid}\n",
        "flowchart TB\n",
        "\n",
        "A[Input Data X]\n",
        "Y[Response: y]\n",
        "\n",
        "subgraph TTR[TransformedTargetRegressor]\n",
        "direction TB\n",
        "\n",
        "Y --> Y1[Center y]\n",
        "Y1 --> P\n",
        "\n",
        "subgraph P[Pipeline]\n",
        "direction TB\n",
        "\n",
        "B[ColumnTransformer]\n",
        "\n",
        "subgraph N[Numeric features: x1, x2]\n",
        "direction TB\n",
        "N1[StandardScaler]\n",
        "N2[PolynomialFeatures]\n",
        "N1 --> N2\n",
        "end\n",
        "\n",
        "subgraph C[Categorical features: group]\n",
        "direction TB\n",
        "C1[OneHotEncoder]\n",
        "end\n",
        "\n",
        "B --> N\n",
        "B --> C\n",
        "\n",
        "N2 --> D[Combined feature matrix]\n",
        "C1 --> D\n",
        "D --> E[LinearRegression]\n",
        "end\n",
        "\n",
        "P --> H[Predictions on centered y-scale]\n",
        "H --> I[Reverse y transformation]\n",
        "end\n",
        "\n",
        "A --> B\n",
        "I --> O[Predictions on original y-scale]\n",
        "```\n"
      ],
      "id": "87fecd4d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer, TransformedTargetRegressor\n",
        "from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder, PolynomialFeatures\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "numeric_features = [\"x1\", \"x2\"]\n",
        "categorical_features = [\"group\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "numeric_pipe = Pipeline([(\"scaler\", StandardScaler()), (\"poly\", PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False))])\n",
        "\n",
        "preprocessor = ColumnTransformer(\n",
        "    [(\"num\", numeric_pipe, numeric_features), (\"cat\", OneHotEncoder(drop=\"first\"), categorical_features)]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "pipe = Pipeline([(\"preprocess\", preprocessor), (\"model\", LinearRegression())])\n",
        "model = TransformedTargetRegressor(regressor=pipe, transformer=StandardScaler(with_std=False))"
      ],
      "id": "3c23334d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The entire `pipeline` is trained on the training set and then evaluated on the validation set, where the training and validation sets are determined by cross-validation.\n"
      ],
      "id": "e52bbf3a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_validate\n",
        "\n",
        "cv_result = cross_validate(\n",
        "    model, X, y, cv=KFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=1), scoring=[\"r2\", \"neg_mean_squared_error\"]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"test R2: {cv_result['test_r2'].mean()}\")\n",
        "print(f\"test neg mse: {cv_result['test_neg_mean_squared_error'].mean()}\")"
      ],
      "id": "655ff46c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</details>"
      ],
      "id": "9f35c862"
    }
